== 4. PROVENANCE — the point of the D-025 trial. Ruling in three parts. ==

--- 4a. the reds are SOUND: the reproducible-pre-fix-state class ---
The header `# tree: tree:4f3a0d4ffd965a051a0fc79170bb2a05b13bfcb8` is not decoration and not
a git object — I chased it. `runRed` (lifecycle.ts:580-607) stamps
`sourceTreeHash(repo, [<officina>, .bisellium, ...manifest.source_excludes], "HEAD")`, prefixed
`dirty:` or `tree:` by `isDirtyOutside`. Recomputed independently:

    sourceTreeHash(worktree, ["studio",".bisellium","examples/"], ccb19f8) = 4f3a0d4ffd96...
    sourceTreeHash(worktree, [...],                     63ae1fa/f2f0aa6/HEAD) = 02b00a975618...
    (`git rev-parse ccb19f8^{tree}` = 4fc91a4f... — a DIFFERENT hash. The red does not name a
     git tree; it names the source tree with the officina's own bookkeeping excluded, which is
     the house contract: "a certificate names the source tree, not the officina's bookkeeping.")

So `4f3a0d4f` IS ccb19f8 — harness present, fix absent — and the `tree:` (not `dirty:`) prefix
is a live claim that nothing outside the officina was modified at record time. That claim is
checkable and it checks out.

Then I did not take the transcripts on trust. I reconstructed the state and re-ran the two
recorded commands:

    git show ccb19f8:packages/cli/src/lifecycle.test.ts > packages/cli/src/lifecycle.test.ts
    node scripts/w079-mutation-check.mjs 1   -> exit 1  "behaviour 1 mutation SURVIVED"  67 PASS / 0 FAIL
    node scripts/w079-mutation-check.mjs 2   -> exit 1  "behaviour 2 mutation SURVIVED"  67 PASS / 0 FAIL

    diff (committed 01.log body) (my transcript)  -> IDENTICAL
    diff (committed 02.log body) (my transcript)  -> IDENTICAL

Byte-identical, modulo blank lines, for both behaviours. This is the strongest red evidence
available: not "the builder reports it failed" but "the state is named, and any third party
who reaches that state reproduces the bytes." It is not backfilled either — the state it names
sits before the fix commit in this branch's recorded history, and the content is DERIVABLE from
the tree rather than asserted about it. Evidence produced, not backfilled: upheld.

The two bodies share 76 of 77 lines, differing only on the `mutation SURVIVED (<label>)` line.
`opus.red_content` blocks only on byte-identical-after-header-strip, so no block — and
substantively the identity IS the defect: the unstrengthened suite passes everything in both
cases, and the one differing line is the whole payload. Distinct evidence for distinct guards.

--- 4b. the proxy-execution boundary IS a finding, and it is SANDBOX CONFIGURATION ---
Two commits (ccb19f8, 63ae1fa) carry builder-sol's verbatim messages and co-author trailer but
`edckt` as author/committer; the reds carry `sella: builder-sol` on a run the orchestrator's
runtime spawned. Both disclosed in-flow, the re-record disclosed in 2016be8's message. This is
a real gap in evidentiary independence and I am recording it as such.

It is NOT attributable to builder-tier capability, and therefore NOT a D-025 kill-clause
trigger. The discriminator: every artifact that required MODEL capability is builder-sol's, and
every one of them is correct on the first pass — the mutation harness (exact-string anchors with
a `matches !== 1` assertion, restore in `finally`, a post-restore byte comparison that throws,
inverted exit semantics for test-strengthening evidence), the two mutation strings (they match
lifecycle.ts:1013-1016 and :1097-1103 exactly; anchor count 1 each, verified by the script
firing clean twice), the token argument, the propagation row, house style, prettier-clean,
typecheck-clean, and zero bytes into `packages/commands/`. 69/69 and both mutations dead, first
round, no fix rounds.

What builder-sol could not do was `spawnSync` a subprocess (EPERM), write `.git`, and read git
state. Those are three OS-level permission denials, not three reasoning failures. D-025's kill
clause asks whether the tier can BUILD; a sandbox that denies `fork()` measures the harness's
configuration. Fixable environment fact, with a named fix: give the codex sandbox
workspace-write on the worktree plus subprocess spawn, and both proxies vanish without touching
the model. File as a D-025 environment row, not a kill.

--- 4c. the rule the boundary is survivable UNDER ---
Proxy execution costs exactly one thing: the `sella: builder-sol` header on a log another
runtime produced is a claim about authorship that the log cannot itself support. That is
survivable here only because the evidence is REPRODUCIBLE — I re-derived it in §4a without
trusting the header at all. Had the red been a bare assertion, or had it kept its original
`tree: unknown`, the proxy boundary would have left nothing to check and the correct verdict
would have been FAIL. The generalisable rule, offered for the D-025 retro:

  proxy-executed evidence is admissible IF AND ONLY IF it names a state a third party can
  reach independently. Otherwise it is hearsay.

The original metadata-only logs (`tree: unknown`, 144 bytes, no command output) failed that
test; the re-record at a clean, named tree passes it. Terra's census caught the first shape and
the re-record is the right remedy, done in the right direction — re-recorded AT the pre-fix
state, not reconstructed FROM the post-fix one.

== 5. FINDINGS ==

F1 (advisory · CLASS, 2 instances · not the builder's, not fixable by a fix round)
  W-079's spec gate is signed by the sella that then built it:
    probationes: { spec: { sella: builder-sol, status: passed, ... } }
  The brief's own first line says "Spec of record, signed by the architect, 2026-09-25", and
  qa.md §9 (P-008, option b) says specs are signed by the architect. The architect's signature
  is therefore not on the record, and a builder stamped its own gate.
  I searched every `studio/opera/*.md` front matter for `spec: { sella: <id>` and
  cross-referenced each against its `review:` signer. 12 non-architect spec signers, in three
  sub-classes:
    guest x8   W-022..W-029   pre-P-008 (adopted 2026-09-20), historical
    qa-lead x2 W-030, W-031   ALREADY CAUGHT by check's `process.cascade`
    builder x2 W-062 (builder-b), W-079 (builder-sol)   NOT CAUGHT BY ANYTHING
  The builder sub-class is the live one and W-079 is its second instance. The gap is concrete:
  `process.cascade` fires only when the SAME sella appears on spec and review, so
  spec=builder-b/review=qa-lead and spec=builder-sol/review=qa-lead both slip through. Per the
  house rule that process enforcement is a check rule and never a memory, the remedy is a rule
  that refuses a spec gate signed by a `builder-*` sella (or, strictly per P-008, by any sella
  other than the architect). Not a fix round on this opus: the builder has no lever on
  front matter, and `opera/` bookkeeping is never edited by hand.

F2 (advisory · brief wording, not the build)
  Acceptance criterion 4 — "`git diff --stat` names only `packages/cli/src/lifecycle.test.ts`
  and the new script" — is literally unmet: the diff is 8 files. The other 6 are
  `studio/opera/W-079.md`, three `studio/ci/W-079-*-02b00a97.log`, and the two reds — all
  CLI-written officina bookkeeping that every opus necessarily produces and that the house
  rules forbid editing by hand. The criterion means "no SOURCE file other than these two",
  which IS satisfied (`packages/commands/` zero diff, verified). Brief wording to tighten;
  no defect in the opus.

F3 (NOT a finding — recording it so it is not rediscovered)
  `git diff --check` reports "new blank line at EOF" on all five new CI/red logs. I counted the
  whole corpus: 361 of 722 `studio/ci/**/*.log` on origin/master already end in a blank line.
  It is an artifact of the log writers (`runRed`'s `${header}\n\n${output}` and the pipeline's
  tail join), half the corpus wide, and hand-trimming a `ci/*.log` is explicitly forbidden.
  If anyone wants it gone it is a one-line change in the writers and its own opus.

F4 (NOT a finding)
  The `traditio:` block builder-sol added to W-079.md mid-build via `bisellium handoff` is the
  CLI's own verb writing its own field — exactly how a builder is supposed to hand off, and the
  opposite of hand-edited front matter. `bisellium check` is quiet on it.
  The one advisory check does raise on this record — `probatio.certifies: gate "spec" evidence
  has no identity (certifies)` — which is the spec gate's shape, shared with every other opus's
  spec gate; not W-079's doing.

== 6. verdict ==
PASS. Every claim in the brief was re-measured here rather than read off a log: 67 pre-fix and
69 post-fix at the two named trees, both mutations dead on HEAD and both alive at ccb19f8 with
transcripts identical to the committed reds, zero diff into `packages/commands/`, all four
suites green, check 0 blocking. The three advisories are orchestration bookkeeping (F1), brief
wording (F2) and a corpus-wide log artifact (F3) — none of them the builder's work and none
fixable by a round dispatched at builder-sol.

On D-025: the trial SUCCEEDS on capability and FAILS on isolation for an environmental reason.
builder-sol produced a first-pass-correct test-only opus with a non-trivial mutation harness and
no fix rounds. The proxy commits and proxy-executed reds are a sandbox-permission defect with a
named one-line remedy, not a tier defect, so the kill clause is not triggered. Carry F1 and the
§4c admissibility rule into the retro.
